{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     media — one responsive image, lazy-loaded, revealed on scroll.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     EVERY image on the site goes through this partial. That is the point: it
     is the only place `loading`, `decoding`, `srcset`, `sizes` and the reveal
     wrapper are decided, so they cannot be right in five templates and wrong
     in the sixth.

     PARAMETERS
       src     the image URL (required)
       alt     alternative text. Pass "" deliberately for a decorative image
               whose link is already labelled — see `decorative` below.
       sizes   the `sizes` attribute. Defaults to full-viewport, which is
               correct for a hero and wrong for a card, so cards pass their
               own.
       ratio   an aspect-ratio class hook, e.g. "16x9"
       eager   truthy → load immediately and do NOT reveal. Use for the ONE
               image above the fold; see below.
       decorative  truthy → aria-hidden, empty alt

     ── Lazy, but not the first image ──────────────────────────────────────
     `loading="lazy"` on an image that is already in the viewport DELAYS it:
     the browser must lay the page out before it knows the image is visible,
     so the request starts later than an eager one would. On a post hero that
     is a direct hit to Largest Contentful Paint — the single metric a theme
     is judged on. Hence `eager`, and hence heroes passing it.

     ── The reveal ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `.ns-reveal` is NSDS's own clip-reveal: the wrapper hides overflow and
     the inner element rises into it. `assets/js/type-fx.js` observes
     `.ns-reveal` and sets `[data-fx-in]` when it scrolls into view, so this
     needs no theme JavaScript at all.

     Three things make it safe rather than decorative-at-the-cost-of-content:

       · The hidden start state lives inside `@media (scripting: enabled)`,
         so with JS off the image is simply THERE rather than translated off
         its own box waiting for an observer that will never run.
       · The animation's FINAL state is the correct one, so
         prefers-reduced-motion — which flattens every animation globally —
         leaves a visible image rather than an invisible one.
       · It draws once. A thing that animates every time it scrolls past is
         an advertisement.

     An eager image skips the wrapper entirely: revealing the hero means
     hiding the largest thing on the page until a script runs, which is the
     opposite of what `eager` is for.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#if src}}
    {{#if eager}}
        <img class="sp-media{{#if ratio}} sp-media--{{ratio}}{{/if}}"
             srcset="{{img_url src size="s"}} 300w,
                     {{img_url src size="m"}} 600w,
                     {{img_url src size="l"}} 1000w,
                     {{img_url src size="xl"}} 2000w"
             sizes="{{#if sizes}}{{sizes}}{{else}}100vw{{/if}}"
             src="{{img_url src size="l"}}"
             alt="{{alt}}"
             fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"
             {{#if decorative}}aria-hidden="true"{{/if}}>
    {{else}}
        <span class="ns-reveal sp-media-wrap">
            <span>
                <img class="sp-media{{#if ratio}} sp-media--{{ratio}}{{/if}}"
                     srcset="{{img_url src size="s"}} 300w,
                             {{img_url src size="m"}} 600w,
                             {{img_url src size="l"}} 1000w,
                             {{img_url src size="xl"}} 2000w"
                     sizes="{{#if sizes}}{{sizes}}{{else}}100vw{{/if}}"
                     src="{{img_url src size="m"}}"
                     alt="{{alt}}"
                     loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                     {{#if decorative}}aria-hidden="true"{{/if}}>
            </span>
        </span>
    {{/if}}
{{/if}}
